$DIR/main.p:15:10: cannot pass a function literal that captures n (main.p:15) to wg.Task, which starts a task with it: its closure record lives in the starting frame (spec section 12.12)
    A function literal that uses variables of the function around it reaches
    them in that function's stack frame. A task statement may start it after
    that function has returned, when the frame is gone.
    Start the literal with a sync.Scope instead: s.Task(func() {...}) waits for
    it before s's block ends, so it may use the function's variables, a Mutex or
    a WaitGroup among them. Or pass the values the literal needs as arguments:
    they are copied when the task statement runs. Do not pass a Mutex, a
    WaitGroup or another sync type this way, since the copy is a different one.
    The compiler reports this where it sees the literal itself: written in the
    task statement, or held by a local variable whose only assignment is its
    declaration. Library functions that start a task with a func argument, such
    as sync.WaitGroup.Task and time.AfterFunc, follow the same rule, as does a
    function of the program that passes a func parameter on to a task. A method
    value is E2594.
    Go moves captured variables to the heap. Plutonium never allocates behind
    the program's back, so captured variables stay in their frame (spec section
    11.5).
    Pass the values as arguments:
        for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
            task func(i int, out chan int) {
                out <- i * i
            }(i, out)
        }
    (see "Task statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.12)
    (run "pluto explain E2590" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:18:10: cannot pass work to wg.Task, which starts a task with it: it holds a function literal that captures n (main.p:17), whose closure record lives in the starting frame (spec section 12.12)
    (explained above; run "pluto explain E2590" for more)

$DIR/main.p:21:30: cannot pass the method value c.add to time.AfterFunc, which starts a task with it: its closure record lives in the starting frame (spec section 12.12)
    A function literal that captures variables, and a method value such as
    c.handle, keep their closure record (the code to call, with the captured
    variables or a copy of the receiver) in the frame of the function that makes
    them. A func value points to that record. A task statement copies its
    function and arguments, but copying a func value copies only the pointer, so
    the task may call through it after the frame is gone.
    The compiler reports a method value, or a closure that a function builds in
    the region this frame provides, by this code where it sees it: passed to a
    task statement, or held by a local variable assigned only once. A capturing
    function literal seen this way is E2590. A func value that reaches the task
    start any other way (through a parameter, a field, an element, or a variable
    assigned twice) is reported by the check of views of the frame (E3003,
    E3004), since it may be such a closure.
    Library functions that start a task with a func argument, such as
    sync.WaitGroup.Task and time.AfterFunc, follow the same rule and the
    compiler reports them the same way.
    To let the task use the function's variables, start a function literal with
    a sync.Scope declared in the function instead: s.Task(func() {...}) waits
    for it before s's block ends.
    Go moves captured variables and the receivers of method values to the heap.
    Plutonium never allocates behind the program's back, so the record stays in
    its frame (spec section 11.5).
    Pass the values the function needs, and start a function that takes them:
        task func(n int, out chan int) {
            out <- n * n
        }(n, out)
    (see "Task statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.12)
    (run "pluto explain E2594" for a longer explanation)

